T41	Quotation 10110 10145	"Whatever you say is always right,"
T42	Quotation 10161 10520	"and therefore I suppose, and believe, and hope it must be so; but otherwise I could not have imagined it.  It is so much beyond any thing I deserve.  Mr. Elton, who might marry any body! There cannot be two opinions about him.  He is so very superior.  Only think of those sweet verses-- 'To Miss --.' Dear me, how clever!-- Could it really be meant for me?"
T43	Cue 10146 10151	cried
R49	Cueing Arg1:T43 Arg2:T41	
T44	Entity 10152 10159	Harriet
R50	Cueing Arg1:T43 Arg2:T42	
R51	Speaker Arg1:T44 Arg2:T41	
R52	Speaker Arg1:T44 Arg2:T42	
R53	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T41	
R54	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T42	
T45	Quotation 10521 10746	"I cannot make a question, or listen to a question about that.  It is a certainty.  Receive it on my judgment.  It is a sort of prologue to the play, a motto to the chapter; and will be soon followed by matter-of-fact prose."
T46	Quotation 10747 10894	"It is a sort of thing which nobody could have expected.  I am sure, a month ago, I had no more idea myself!-- The strangest things do take place!"
T47	Quotation 10895 11645	"When Miss Smiths and Mr. Eltons get acquainted-- they do indeed-- and really it is strange; it is out of the common course that what is so evidently, so palpably desirable-- what courts the pre-arrangement of other people, should so immediately shape itself into the proper form. You and Mr. Elton are by situation called together; you belong to one another by every circumstance of your respective homes.  Your marrying will be equal to the match at Randalls.  There does seem to be a something in the air of Hartfield which gives love exactly the right direction, and sends it into the very channel where it ought to flow.  The course of true love never did run smooth--  A Hartfield edition of Shakespeare would have a long note on that passage."
T48	Quotation 11646 12609	"That Mr. Elton should really be in love with me,-- me, of all people, who did not know him, to speak to him, at Michaelmas! And he, the very handsomest man that ever was, and a man that every body looks up to, quite like Mr. Knightley! His company so sought after, that every body says he need not eat a single meal by himself if he does not chuse it; that he has more invitations than there are days in the week.  And so excellent in the Church! Miss Nash has put down all the texts he has ever preached from since he came to Highbury.  Dear me! When I look back to the first time I saw him! How little did I think!-- The two Abbots and I ran into the front room and peeped through the blind when we heard he was going by, and Miss Nash came and scolded us away, and staid to look through herself; however, she called me back presently, and let me look too, which was very good-natured. And how beautiful we thought he looked!  He was arm-in-arm with Mr. Cole."
T49	Quotation 12610 13248	"This is an alliance which, whoever-- whatever your friends may be, must be agreeable to them, provided at least they have common sense; and we are not to be addressing our conduct to fools.  If they are anxious to see you happily married, here is a man whose amiable character gives every assurance of it;-- if they wish to have you settled in the same country and circle which they have chosen to place you in, here it will be accomplished; and if their only object is that you should, in the common phrase, be well married, here is the comfortable fortune, the respectable establishment, the rise in the world which must satisfy them."
T50	Quotation 13249 13480	"Yes, very true.  How nicely you talk; I love to hear you.  You understand every thing.  You and Mr. Elton are one as clever as the other.  This charade!-- If I had studied a twelvemonth, I could never have made any thing like it."
T51	Quotation 13481 13560	"I thought he meant to try his skill, by his manner of declining it yesterday."
T52	Quotation 13561 13629	"I do think it is, without exception, the best charade I ever read."
T53	Quotation 13630 13680	"I never read one more to the purpose, certainly."
T54	Quotation 13681 13736	"It is as long again as almost all we have had before."
T55	Quotation 13737 13843	"I do not consider its length as particularly in its favour.  Such things in general cannot be too short."
T56	Quotation 13949 13967	"It is one thing,"
T57	Quotation 14013 14240	"to have very good sense in a common way, like every body else, and if there is any thing to say, to sit down and write a letter, and say just what you must, in a short way; and another, to write verses and charades like this."
T58	Quotation 14318 14337	"Such sweet lines!"
T59	Cue 14338 14347	continued
T60	Entity 14241 14245	Emma
T61	Entity 14348 14355	Harriet
T62	Cue 13968 13972	said
T63	Entity 13973 13976	she
T64	Quotation 14358 14503	"these two last!-- But how shall I ever be able to return the paper, or say I have found it out?-- Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what can we do about that?
T65	Quotation 14505 14768	"Leave it to me.  You do nothing.  He will be here this evening, I dare say, and then I will give it him back, and some nonsense or other will pass between us, and you shall not be committed.-- Your soft eyes shall chuse their own time for beaming.  Trust to me."
T66	Quotation 14769 14904	"Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what a pity that I must not write this beautiful charade into my book! I am sure I have not got one half so good."
T67	Quotation 14905 15003	"Leave out the two last lines, and there is no reason why you should not write it into your book."
