R960	Speaker Arg1:T765 Arg2:T763	
R961	Addressee Arg1:T761 Arg2:T763	
T766	Quotation 182443 182965	"I had.  Once, I felt the fire rather too much; but then I moved back my chair a little, a very little, and it did not disturb me. Miss Bates was very chatty and good-humoured, as she always is, though she speaks rather too quick.  However, she is very agreeable, and Mrs. Bates too, in a different way.  I like old friends; and Miss Jane Fairfax is a very pretty sort of young lady, a very pretty and a very well-behaved young lady indeed.  She must have found the evening agreeable, Mr. Knightley, because she had Emma."
R963	Cueing Arg1:T764 Arg2:T766	
R962	Addressee Arg1:T761 Arg2:T766	
R964	Speaker Arg1:T765 Arg2:T766	
T767	Quotation 182966 183018	"True, sir; and Emma, because she had Miss Fairfax."
T768	Quotation 183151 183294	"She is a sort of elegant creature that one cannot keep one's eyes from.  I am always watching her to admire; and I do pity her from my heart."
R965	Addressee Arg1:T765 Arg2:T767	
R966	Speaker Arg1:T761 Arg2:T767	
T769	Entity 183019 183023	Emma
R967	Speaker Arg1:T769 Arg2:T768	
T770	Entity 183028 183031	his
R968	Addressee Arg1:T770 Arg2:T768	
T771	Quotation 183465 184113	"It is a great pity that their circumstances should be so confined!  a great pity indeed! and I have often wished-- but it is so little one can venture to do-- small, trifling presents, of any thing uncommon-- Now we have killed a porker, and Emma thinks of sending them a loin or a leg; it is very small and delicate-- Hartfield pork is not like any other pork-- but still it is pork-- and, my dear Emma, unless one could be sure of their making it into steaks, nicely fried, as ours are fried, without the smallest grease, and not roast it, for no stomach can bear roast pork -- I think we had better send the leg-- do not you think so, my dear?"
T772	Entity 183407 183420	Mr. Woodhouse
T773	Cue 183458 183462	said
T774	Entity 183295 183308	Mr. Knightley
R969	Cueing Arg1:T773 Arg2:T771	
R970	Addressee Arg1:T774 Arg2:T771	
R971	Speaker Arg1:T772 Arg2:T771	
T775	Quotation 184114 184316	"My dear papa, I sent the whole hind-quarter. I knew you would wish it. There will be the leg to be salted, you know, which is so very nice, and the loin to be dressed directly in any manner they like."
T776	Quotation 184317 184663	"That's right, my dear, very right.  I had not thought of it before, but that is the best way.  They must not over-salt the leg; and then, if it is not over-salted, and if it is very thoroughly boiled, just as Serle boils ours, and eaten very moderately of, with a boiled turnip, and a little carrot or parsnip, I do not consider it unwholesome."
T777	Quotation 184664 184671	"Emma,"
T778	Cue 184672 184676	said
T779	Entity 184677 184690	Mr. Knightley
T780	Quotation 184702 184823	"I have a piece of news for you. You like news-- and I heard an article in my way hither that I think will interest you."
T781	Quotation 184824 184937	"News!  Oh! yes, I always like news.  What is it?-- why do you smile so?-- where did you hear it?-- at Randalls?"
T782	Entity 184938 184940	He
T783	Cue 184941 184961	had time only to say
T784	Quotation 184963 185016	"No, not at Randalls; I have not been near Randalls,"
T785	Entity 185181 185194	Mr. Knightley
T786	Quotation 185301 185522	"Oh! my dear sir, how are you this morning?  My dear Miss Woodhouse-- I come quite over-powered. Such a beautiful hind-quarter of pork!  You are too bountiful!  Have you heard the news?  Mr. Elton is going to be married."
T787	Quotation 185684 185739	"There is my news: -- I thought it would interest you,"
T788	Cue 185740 185744	said
T789	Entity 185745 185758	Mr. Knightley
T790	Quotation 185846 185876	"But where could you hear it?"
T791	Cue 185877 185882	cried
T792	Entity 185883 185893	Miss Bates
T793	Quotation 185896 186758	"Where could you possibly hear it, Mr. Knightley?  For it is not five minutes since I received Mrs. Cole's note-- no, it cannot be more than five-- or at least ten-- for I had got my bonnet and spencer on, just ready to come out -- I was only gone down to speak to Patty again about the pork-- Jane was standing in the passage-- were not you, Jane?-- for my mother was so afraid that we had not any salting-pan large enough.  So I said I would go down and see, and Jane said, 'Shall I go down instead? for I think you have a little cold, and Patty has been washing the kitchen.'-- 'Oh! my dear,' said I-- well, and just then came the note.  A Miss Hawkins-- that's all I know.  A Miss Hawkins of Bath.  But, Mr. Knightley, how could you possibly have heard it? for the very moment Mr. Cole told Mrs. Cole of it, she sat down and wrote to me.  A Miss Hawkins -- "
T794	Quotation 186759 186898	"I was with Mr. Cole on business an hour and a half ago.  He had just read Elton's letter as I was shewn in, and handed it to me directly."
T795	Quotation 186899 187157	"Well! that is quite -- I suppose there never was a piece of news more generally interesting.  My dear sir, you really are too bountiful.  My mother desires her very best compliments and regards, and a thousand thanks, and says you really quite oppress her."
T796	Quotation 187158 187191	"We consider our Hartfield pork,"
T797	Cue 187192 187199	replied
T798	Entity 187200 187213	Mr. Woodhouse
T799	Quotation 187216 187333	"indeed it certainly is, so very superior to all other pork, that Emma and I cannot have a greater pleasure than -- "
T800	Quotation 187334 187664	"Oh! my dear sir, as my mother says, our friends are only too good to us.  If ever there were people who, without having great wealth themselves, had every thing they could wish for, I am sure it is us. We may well say that 'our lot is cast in a goodly heritage.' Well, Mr. Knightley, and so you actually saw the letter; well -- "
T801	Quotation 187665 187737	"It was short-- merely to announce-- but cheerful, exulting, of course."
T802	Quotation 187772 188009	"He had been so fortunate as to -- I forget the precise words-- one has no business to remember them. The information was, as you state, that he was going to be married to a Miss Hawkins.  By his style, I should imagine it just settled."
T803	Quotation 188010 188042	"Mr. Elton going to be married!"
T804	Cue 188043 188047	said
T805	Entity 188048 188052	Emma
T806	Quotation 188082 188135	"He will have every body's wishes for his happiness."
T807	Quotation 188136 188165	"He is very young to settle,"
T808	Cue 188186 188197	observation
T809	Entity 188170 188183	Mr. Woodhouse
T810	Quotation 188200 188322	"He had better not be in a hurry.  He seemed to me very well off as he was.  We were always glad to see him at Hartfield."
T811	Quotation 188323 188368	"A new neighbour for us all, Miss Woodhouse!"
T812	Cue 188369 188373	said
T813	Entity 188374 188384	Miss Bates
T814	Quotation 188396 188620	"my mother is so pleased!-- she says she cannot bear to have the poor old Vicarage without a mistress.  This is great news, indeed. Jane, you have never seen Mr. Elton!-- no wonder that you have such a curiosity to see him."
T815	Quotation 188778 188805	"is he-- is he a tall man?"
T816	Entity 188740 188743	she
T817	Cue 188744 188751	replied
T818	Quotation 188703 188739	"No -- I have never seen Mr. Elton,"
T819	Cue 188840 188845	cried
