T839	Quotation 191185 191257	"Yes, he has been gone just four weeks, as you observe, Miss Woodhouse,"
T840	Cue 191258 191262	said
T841	Entity 191263 191273	Miss Bates
T842	Quotation 191275 192110	"four weeks yesterday. -- A Miss Hawkins!-- Well, I had always rather fancied it would be some young lady hereabouts; not that I ever-- Mrs. Cole once whispered to me-- but I immediately said, 'No, Mr. Elton is a most worthy young man-- but'-- In short, I do not think I am particularly quick at those sort of discoveries.  I do not pretend to it.  What is before me, I see.  At the same time, nobody could wonder if Mr. Elton should have aspired-- Miss Woodhouse lets me chatter on, so good-humouredly. She knows I would not offend for the world.  How does Miss Smith do?  She seems quite recovered now.  Have you heard from Mrs. John Knightley lately?  Oh! those dear little children.  Jane, do you know I always fancy Mr. Dixon like Mr. John Knightley.  I mean in person-- tall, and with that sort of look-- and not very talkative."
T843	Quotation 192111 192168	"Quite wrong, my dear aunt; there is no likeness at all."
T844	Quotation 192169 192344	"Very odd! but one never does form a just idea of any body beforehand. One takes up a notion, and runs away with it.  Mr. Dixon, you say, is not, strictly speaking, handsome?"
T845	Quotation 192345 192423	"Handsome!  Oh! no-- far from it-- certainly plain.  I told you he was plain."
T846	Quotation 192424 192521	"My dear, you said that Miss Campbell would not allow him to be plain, and that you yourself -- "
T847	Quotation 192522 192703	"Oh! as for me, my judgment is worth nothing.  Where I have a regard, I always think a person well-looking. But I gave what I believed the general opinion, when I called him plain."
R1059	Addressee Arg1:T837 Arg2:T842	
R1060	Addressee Arg1:T837 Arg2:T839	
R1061	Cueing Arg1:T840 Arg2:T839	
R1062	Cueing Arg1:T840 Arg2:T842	
R1063	Speaker Arg1:T841 Arg2:T842	
R1064	Speaker Arg1:T841 Arg2:T839	
T848	Quotation 192704 193575	"Well, my dear Jane, I believe we must be running away.  The weather does not look well, and grandmama will be uneasy.  You are too obliging, my dear Miss Woodhouse; but we really must take leave.  This has been a most agreeable piece of news indeed.  I shall just go round by Mrs. Cole's; but I shall not stop three minutes: and, Jane, you had better go home directly -- I would not have you out in a shower!-- We think she is the better for Highbury already.  Thank you, we do indeed. I shall not attempt calling on Mrs. Goddard, for I really do not think she cares for any thing but boiled pork: when we dress the leg it will be another thing.  Good morning to you, my dear sir.  Oh!  Mr. Knightley is coming too.  Well, that is so very! -- I am sure if Jane is tired, you will be so kind as to give her your arm.-- Mr. Elton, and Miss Hawkins!-- Good morning to you."
T849	Quotation 194605 194658	"Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what do you think has happened!"
R1065	Speaker Arg1:T841 Arg2:T848	
R1066	Addressee Arg1:T837 Arg2:T848	
R1067	Speaker Arg1:T837 Arg2:T847	
